    <title>Connect Garmin · Calories</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <a href="/calories/">← Back to your journal</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">GARMIN CONNECT</p>
      <h1>Connect once.<br/>Keep your days in sync.</h1>
      <p>Your cloud worker is configured. Finish the one-time Garmin sign-in on your Mac to start importing daily calories.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Open the connection helper.</strong><p>In Finder, press <kbd>⌘</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>G</kbd>, paste this folder, and open <b>Connect Garmin.command</b>.</p><code>{localFolder}</code></li>
        <li><strong>Sign in privately.</strong><p>Enter your Garmin email and password in the Terminal window. Your password stays hidden while you type. Enter Garmin’s verification code if asked.</p></li>
        <li><strong>Return to your journal.</strong><p>Open Garmin Connect in the journal and choose “Refresh imported data”. The first sign-in imports the past 30 days; the cloud worker then checks today and the previous seven days once an hour.</p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="note">Sync your watch with Garmin Connect first. Today’s predicted burned calories use today’s active calories + yesterday’s resting calories. Completed days use Garmin’s recorded total. Incomplete readings leave the last valid value in place.</p>
      <h2>When Garmin asks you to reconnect</h2>
      <p>Open the same helper and sign in again. The worker stores an encrypted session rather than your password. You can stop imports with “Disconnect Garmin” in the journal; previously imported calorie readings are kept.</p>
      <p>You can change any day to a manual calorie value in “Daily targets”. That override stays in place after future imports.</p>
      <p class="footnote">This personal connection uses an unofficial Garmin client. Garmin can change its sign-in requirements, and GitHub’s hourly jobs can be delayed. The journal shows the last successful import so you can check freshness.</p>
    </main>
